window.GUIDE_DATA = [{"vocab":[{"word":"Agreeableness","definition":"measures the degree to which you are friendly or reserved, cooperative or guarded, flexible or inflexible, trusting or cautious, good-natured or moody, soft-hearted or tough, and tolerant or judgmental."},{"word":"Attitudes","definition":"evaluative statements or \"learned predispositions to respond to an object, person or idea in a favorable or unfavorable way.\""},{"word":"Attribution theory","definition":"demonstrates that individuals tend to decide that a behavior is caused by a particular characteristic or event."},{"word":"Behavior","definition":"is the way in which we conduct ourselves—the way in which we act."},{"word":"Collectivist","definition":"meaning that people define themselves in relation to what is acceptable within their group."},{"word":"Conscientiousness","definition":"represents the degree to which you are dependable, can be counted on, follow through on commitments, and keep promises."},{"word":"Emotional stability","definition":"measures the degree to which you are consistent in how you react to certain events, weigh options before acting, and look at a situation objectively."},{"word":"Expectations","definition":"involve forming an opinion about how we would like an event to unfold, a situation to develop, or a person to act, think, or feel."},{"word":"Extroversion","definition":"assesses the degree to which you are social or antisocial, outgoing or shy, assertive or passive, active or inactive, and talkative or quiet."},{"word":"Fundamental attribution error","definition":"causes individuals to overestimate the impact of internal factors and underestimate the influence of external factors when evaluating the behavior of others."},{"word":"Individualism","definition":"meaning that people define themselves independent of group affiliation."},{"word":"Interest","definition":"is basing our activities and inputs on things that are likeable or appealing to us."},{"word":"Modes of acting","definition":"the course of action you apply in a given situation."},{"word":"Modes of interacting","definition":"the way in which you communicate ideas, opinions, and feelings with others."},{"word":"Modes of thinking","definition":"the way you process the various inputs your brain receives."},{"word":"Motivation","definition":"the drive to pursue one action over another."},{"word":"Openness to experience","definition":"considers whether you are interested in broadening your horizons or limiting them, learning new things or sticking with what you already know, meeting new people or associating with current friends and co-workers, going to new places or restricting yourself to known places."},{"word":"Perception","definition":"is the process by which you select, evaluate, and organize information and make sense of it."},{"word":"Personality","definition":"describes the relatively stable set of characteristics, tendencies, and temperaments that have been formed by heredity and by social, cultural, and environmental factors."},{"word":"Projection","definition":"is attributing one's own attitudes, characteristics, or shortcomings to others."},{"word":"Selective perception","definition":"is interpreting information for meaning and accuracy, and discarding information that is threatening or not relevant."},{"word":"Self-analysis","definition":"requires you to step back and observe (as objectively as possible) the factors that influence your behaviors, attitudes, thoughts, or interactions."},{"word":"Self-awareness","definition":"is \"the capacity for introspection and the ability to reconcile oneself as an individual separate from the environment and other individuals.\""},{"word":"Self-disclosure","definition":"sharing your thoughts, feelings, and ideas with others without self-deception, without distortion."},{"word":"Self-monitoring","definition":"is the ability to regulate your thinking before speaking or acting."},{"word":"Self-serving bias","definition":"causes us to overestimate internal factors for successes and blame external factors for failures."},{"word":"Social mirror","definition":"we gain perceptions of ourselves as a result of what other people say about us or how they react to us."},{"word":"Stereotyping","definition":"is making assumptions about an individual or a group based on generalized judgments rather than on facts."},{"word":"Diverse Experiences","definition":"another way of increasing self-awareness is through acquiring multiple experiences in diverse situations and with diverse others."}],"title":"Journey into Self-awareness","id":1},{"vocab":[{"word":"Authenticity","definition":"Authenticity is becoming a \"currency of exchange\" in online communications."},{"word":"Blind area","definition":"denotes information others are aware of but we are not."},{"word":"Competence","definition":"knowledge and ability."},{"word":"Computer-mediated communication (CMC)","definition":"communication occurring through the use of electronic devices, as opposed to face-to-face (FTF) communication."},{"word":"Consistency","definition":"conformity with previous practice; good judgment in handling situations."},{"word":"Self-description","definition":"is the disclosure of nonthreatening information such as age, address, major, or organization for which you work."},{"word":"Self-disclosure","definition":"means making the self known to others, letting others know what you think, feel, and want. It is revealing personal information to another that results in an enhanced and trusting personal or business relationship."},{"word":"Face-to-face (FTF) communication","definition":"communication occurring face to face, as opposed to computer-mediated communication (CMC)."},{"word":"Hidden area","definition":"comprises information we know about ourselves but is hidden from others."},{"word":"Integrity","definition":"honesty and sincerity."},{"word":"Johari Window","definition":"helps us understand how well we know ourselves and how much of ourselves we let others know."},{"word":"Loyalty","definition":"faithfulness to your friends and ideals."},{"word":"Open area","definition":"consists of information about us that both we and others know, such as our name, job title or role, level in the organization, and possibly something about our personal life such as our marital status or the college from which you graduated."},{"word":"Openness","definition":"welcomes new ideas; willing to share ideas with others."},{"word":"Social networking sites (SNS)","definition":"like LinkedIn, Facebook, or Twitter."},{"word":"Transparency","definition":"Speak openly and transparently without hidden agendas."},{"word":"Trust","definition":"is one's faith or belief in the integrity or reliability of another person or thing. Trust means confidence."},{"word":"Unknown area","definition":"contains information that neither we nor others know."}],"title":"Self-disclosure and Trust","id":2},{"vocab":[{"word":"Code of ethics","definition":"A written statement of values and guidelines for how to treat employees and customers."},{"word":"Conventional level","definition":"Individuals at the second level, or conventional level of reasoning, have a basic understanding of the need to conform to societal standards, realizing that norms and conventions are necessary to uphold society."},{"word":"Decision-making model","definition":"Frameworks that employees can use to help make decisions about ethical actions by following a short, step-by-step list of rules."},{"word":"Ethical audit","definition":"A broad-based, agreed-upon system that lets an organization consistently focus and refocus on its values and whether its performance is meeting the standards it professes."},{"word":"Ethical barometer","definition":"Each person possesses a different ethical barometer that stems from his or her experience and background."},{"word":"Ethical decision making","definition":"involves recognizing that an issue involves an ethical question, making an ethical judgment, deciding to do the ethical thing, and actually acting ethically."},{"word":"Ethical dilemmas","definition":"are situations where we are faced with setting goals or making decisions that will be based largely on judgments and determinations rather than on indisputable facts."},{"word":"Ethics","definition":"involves moral principles that people use to guide their behavior by separating right from wrong."},{"word":"Ethics test","definition":"is a series of questions that aids employees in making well-considered judgments about a situation before making a decision."},{"word":"Goal","definition":"is a level of proficiency or standard of behavior we wish to attain within a specified period of time."},{"word":"Goal setting","definition":"is a way of identifying your work/life priorities and developing strategies for attaining personal and professional objectives."},{"word":"Instrumental values","definition":"are the \"how's\" of goal setting—the standards of behavior by which we achieve desired ends."},{"word":"Intangible values","definition":"are concepts rather than things."},{"word":"Moral development","definition":"Lawrence Kohlberg identified six stages of moral development and reasoning, which he grouped into three major levels."},{"word":"Morality","definition":"or a person's belief about his or her obligations."},{"word":"Postconventional level","definition":"is characterized by reasoning based on personal values and principles."},{"word":"Preconventional level","definition":"a person's moral judgments are characterized by concrete, individual perspectives."},{"word":"SMART goals","definition":"SMART refers to a goal that is specific, measurable, attainable, realistic, and has a time frame."},{"word":"Stakeholders","definition":"Those with an interest in the outcome of the decision."},{"word":"Tangible values","definition":"are things you can see, feel, or hold."},{"word":"Terminal values","definition":"are the \"what's\"—the end states or goals that we would like to achieve during our lifetime."},{"word":"Values","definition":"are concepts or beliefs about desirable outcomes that transcend specific situations and guide your selection or evaluation of behaviors and events."}],"title":"Establishing Goals Consistent with Your Values and Ethics","id":3},{"vocab":[{"word":"Anticipatory fear","definition":"has two distinct components: anxiety, a preoccupation with an anticipated threat, and worry, the internal struggle to devise a strategy to escape the threat."},{"word":"Anxiety","definition":"a preoccupation with an anticipated threat."},{"word":"Bad fear","definition":"is a misreading of a situation."},{"word":"Behavioral strategies","definition":"focus on changing behavior to improve one's situation and can include time management, problem solving, goal planning, and healthy lifestyle adjustment."},{"word":"Cognitive strategies","definition":"are designed to influence our thinking, emotions, and well-being."},{"word":"Distress","definition":"Bad stress, or distress, is negative and results in debilitating effects."},{"word":"Emotional intelligence (EQ)","definition":"Emotional intelligence has five components: Self-awareness—emotional awareness, accurate self-assessment, self-confidence, ability to recognize emotions and their effects on you and others. Self-regulation—self-control, trustworthiness, conscientiousness, adaptability, innovation, ability to manage disruptive emotions and impulses. Motivation—zeal, achievement drive, commitment, initiative, optimism, and the ability to remain persistent in the face of adversity. Empathy—understanding others, service orientation, developing others, leveraging diversity, political awareness, the ability to read and respond to others' feelings. Social skills—interacting smoothly, managing interpersonal relationships, handling emotional responses to others, influence, communication, the ability to build bonds with others."},{"word":"Eustress","definition":"Good stress, or eustress, is positive, presents opportunity for personal growth or satisfaction, and pushes people to higher performance."},{"word":"Failure to delegate","definition":"the inability to assign task responsibility to others."},{"word":"Fear","definition":"is one of the most basic of human emotions. It is part of your body's natural alarm system that helps you react physically and mentally to danger and threat or the anticipation of danger and threat."},{"word":"Fear of failure","definition":"refers to the disposition to avoid an undertaking because of the anticipation of shame and humiliation upon failing."},{"word":"Good fear","definition":"maintains your alertness and vigilance. Based on knowledge, reason, and instincts, good fear keeps you from danger or harmful situations."},{"word":"Organizational stress","definition":"or work-related stress is the \"adverse reaction people have to excessive pressures or other types of demands placed on them.\""},{"word":"Physiological stress","definition":"A physiological stress response is one in which physical problems develop as a result of mental anguish."},{"word":"Psychological stress","definition":"Depression, sudden bursts of violence or anger, anxiety, chemical dependency, alcohol abuse, overeating, withdrawal, and phobias are examples of psychological reactions to stressful situations."},{"word":"Self-management","definition":"is the effort to exert control over your decision making and behavior by assessing problems and developing specific goals and strategies to structure your environment to address those problems."},{"word":"Self-regulation","definition":"the long-term commitment to control and regulate your impulses, emotions, performance, and other behaviors."},{"word":"Stress","definition":"the nonspecific response of the body to any demand made upon it."},{"word":"Stress levels","definition":"refer to the degree to which you let the source of the stress affect you."},{"word":"Stress management","definition":"refers to your efforts to change perceptions and behavior when external and internal demands exceed personal resources."},{"word":"Stress tolerance","definition":"our ability to cope with changing conditions and unexpected negative events."},{"word":"Stressor","definition":"is a situation, activity, or person that causes you to feel stressed, out of control, or frazzled."},{"word":"Time famine","definition":"or feeling we have too much to do and not enough time to do it."},{"word":"Time management","definition":"is \"behaviors that aim at achieving an effective use of time while performing certain goal-directed activities.\""},{"word":"Worry","definition":"the internal struggle to devise a strategy to escape the threat."}],"title":"Self-management","id":4},{"vocab":[{"word":"Affirmative action","definition":"programs seek to remedy past discrimination by implementing proactive steps based on race or gender to prevent current or future discrimination."},{"word":"Backlash","definition":"negative reaction to members of previously underrepresented groups gaining power and influence, which leads to fear, resentment, and reverse discrimination."},{"word":"Blaming the victim","definition":"making incorrect causal attributions."},{"word":"Deep-level diversity","definition":"attributes are not easily identifiable at first glance; they are communicated through verbal and nonverbal channels."},{"word":"Discrimination","definition":"barring an individual from membership in an organization or from a job because of his or her membership in a particular group."},{"word":"Diversity","definition":"can be defined as any attribute relevant to an individual that creates or reinforces a perception that he or she is different from another individual."},{"word":"Diversity training","definition":"It is estimated that organizations spend over $8 billion annually on diversity training and other diversity-related initiatives."},{"word":"Equal employment opportunity","definition":"policies ensure employment decisions are made without regard to legally protected attributes such as race, color, gender, physical disability, or national origin."},{"word":"Ethnocentrism","definition":"a tendency to regard one's own group, culture, or nation as superior to others."},{"word":"Global mindset","definition":"The global mindset comprises intellectual capital, psychological capital, and social capital."},{"word":"Harassment","definition":"consciously shunning or verbally or physically abusing an individual because of membership in a particular group."},{"word":"Individualism versus collectivism","definition":"refers to how loosely or tightly integrated the society seems, the degree to which the people of the country prefer to act as individuals rather than as members of groups."},{"word":"Long-term versus short-term orientation","definition":"refers to a culture's tendency to focus more on the future or the past, which relates to economic success and values regarding savings and persistence."},{"word":"Managing diversity","definition":"is active and involves proactively supervising, coordinating, and directing the diversity or differences individuals bring to the organization to create an environment that will allow employees to achieve their full potential while ensuring that strategic organizational goals are being met effectively."},{"word":"Masculinity versus femininity","definition":"refers to the degree to which emphasis is placed on assertiveness, relationships, and quality of life."},{"word":"Power distance","definition":"refers to the acceptance (or lack thereof) of unequal power distribution."},{"word":"Prejudice","definition":"unjustified negative attitude toward a person based on his or her membership in a particular group."},{"word":"Stereotypes","definition":"set of beliefs about a group that is applied universally to all members of that group."},{"word":"Surface-level diversity","definition":"attributes such as age, gender, ethnicity/race, disability culture, and language are more easily identifiable."},{"word":"Uncertainty avoidance","definition":"reflects the degree of threat felt when facing ambiguity and risk."},{"word":"Valuing diversity","definition":"is passive. Here the importance or significance of someone's difference does not automatically lead to actions or reactions on the part of the individual valuing the diversity or difference."}],"title":"Understanding and Working with Diverse Others","id":5},{"vocab":[{"word":"Active (or empathetic) listening","definition":"is the most powerful level of listening and requires the largest amount of work. In active listening, communication is a vibrant, two-way process that involves high levels of attentiveness, clarification, and message processing."},{"word":"Attentive listening","definition":"occurs when you are genuinely interested in the speaker's point of view. You are aware something can be learned from the interaction."},{"word":"Chronemics","definition":"is the study of how human beings use and structure time."},{"word":"Environment","definition":"refers to the layout of the space or room, lighting, color scheme, noise, decorations, and so on."},{"word":"Haptics","definition":"refers to the use of touch."},{"word":"Kinesics","definition":"involves body movement, gestures, and posture. This includes eye contact, leaning, and body positioning."},{"word":"Listening","definition":"is the process of hearing someone speak, processing what you're hearing, and demonstrating that you understand the speaker's intent."},{"word":"Nonverbal communication","definition":"is conveying meaning or expressing feelings consciously or subconsciously through means other than words."},{"word":"Paralanguage","definition":"refers to the tone of voice, volume, pitch, or speech rate."},{"word":"Passive listening","definition":"occurs when you are trying to absorb as much of the information presented as possible. You act as a sponge, taking in the information with no or little attempt to process or enhance the messages being sent by the speaker."},{"word":"Processing/evaluating","definition":"involves understanding the meaning, interpreting the implications, evaluating the nonverbal cues, and remembering the message."},{"word":"Proxemics","definition":"is a term to describe the way we use space in communication."},{"word":"Responding","definition":"involves the listener sending the speaker verbal or nonverbal signals that he or she is being heard."},{"word":"Selective perception","definition":"is a process in which you select or pay attention to only that information that adheres to or reinforces your own beliefs, views, or needs, causing severe distortion of messages."},{"word":"Sensing","definition":"involves hearing the words and receiving the nonverbal signals such as body language and facial expressions."}],"title":"Listening and Nonverbal Communication","id":6},{"vocab":[{"word":"Aggressive communication","definition":"includes exerting control over others, humiliating others, dominating, being pushy, always needing to be right, using absolute terms, and blaming others."},{"word":"Assertive communication","definition":"is a form of communication in which you both speak up for your rights and take into account the rights and feelings of others."},{"word":"Communication","definition":"is the act of exchanging thoughts, messages, or information."},{"word":"Communication channel","definition":"The message travels across the communication channel and is then decoded by the receiver, who interprets (decodes) the message according to his or her own perceptions, abilities, and experiences."},{"word":"Decode","definition":"The message travels across the communication channel and is then decoded by the receiver, who interprets (decodes) the message according to his or her own perceptions, abilities, and experiences."},{"word":"Emotions","definition":"are strong feelings such as fear, love, hate, happiness, and anger."},{"word":"Encode","definition":"When a message needs to be sent, a communicator (the sender) encodes a message according to her or his own perceptions, experiences, and abilities."},{"word":"Feedback","definition":"is a response to a sender's behavior, and it influences the manner in which that behavior will continue or not."},{"word":"Filtering","definition":"is the intentional manipulation of information to make it more favorable to the receiver."},{"word":"Information overload","definition":"occurs when the volume of information a person receives exceeds his or her capacity to process it."},{"word":"Information richness","definition":"the potential information-carrying capacity of data."},{"word":"Netiquette","definition":"Some people may expect a high level of formality in email \"netiquette\" (Internet etiquette)."},{"word":"Noise","definition":"is any distortion factor that blocks, disrupts, or distorts the message sent to the receiver, interfering with the communication process."},{"word":"Passive communication","definition":"includes indirectness, avoiding conflict, being easily persuaded/bullied, being overly concerned about pleasing others, and screening or withholding your thoughts and feelings to the extent that the person with whom you're communicating has no idea of your real opinion on the matter being addressed."}],"title":"Communicating Effectively","id":7},{"vocab":[{"word":"ACE Theory","definition":"Reardon's ACE Theory. She suggests that people use three criteria to determine whether to respond to a persuader's arguments: appropriateness, consistency, and effectiveness."},{"word":"Appropriateness (ACE)","definition":"the right thing to do, based on generally accepted standards or norms, or in some cases, rules of law or morality."},{"word":"Cognitive dissonance","definition":"to explain the tension that exists when individuals' beliefs do not align with their behaviors."},{"word":"Composure","definition":"When you are composed, you are solid and sure, even under pressure."},{"word":"Consensus/social proof","definition":"People often decide what to do based on what they see others doing. Cialdini explains that we \"view a behavior as correct in a given situation to the degree that we see others performing it.\""},{"word":"Consistency (ACE)","definition":"the degree to which the action or belief proposed compares to that of similar others or to their own past behaviors or espoused beliefs."},{"word":"Credibility","definition":"relates to the believability of the message deliverer."},{"word":"Effectiveness (ACE)","definition":"the degree to which an action or idea leads to a desirable state or outcome."},{"word":"Expertise","definition":"To be perceived as credible, you must be very knowledgeable about the subject matter (e.g., product or service you offer) and be able to present your knowledge in a way that compels the listener to adopt a certain point of view."},{"word":"Framing for common ground","definition":"involves vividly describing a perspective or context for consideration (using examples, pictures, stories, or metaphors), establishing boundaries and expectations (e.g., what is and isn't under consideration, and what we can expect from each other), and identifying common ground and benefits of collaborating."},{"word":"Inoculation theory","definition":"persuaders can be effective when they anticipate the objections of the persuadee and address those objections before they arise."},{"word":"Manipulation","definition":"convincing people to believe or adhere to something that is neither in their best interest nor something they would believe or do without the presence of the persuader."},{"word":"Persuasion","definition":"is a form of influence. It is a process of guiding people toward the adoption of a behavior, belief, or attitude that the persuader prefers."},{"word":"Positive impression or appearance","definition":"How we look and dress communicates how we feel about ourselves and our situation and influences how others see us."},{"word":"Reciprocity/obligation","definition":"In general, people want to, or feel obligated to, repay favors and kindness."},{"word":"Scarcity","definition":"People are more motivated to go after that which they perceive is scarce."},{"word":"Trustworthiness","definition":"is acquired over time through personal and professional relationships in which others perceive you as consistent, reliable, and conscientious."}],"title":"Persuading Individuals and Audiences","id":8},{"vocab":[{"word":"Arbitrator","definition":"is a neutral third party who has the legal power to bind both parties to an agreement determined by the arbitrator."},{"word":"BATNA","definition":"according to negotiation experts Fisher and Ury, is the best alternative to a negotiated agreement or the best outcome one party to a negotiation could get should the negotiation neither occur nor conclude."},{"word":"Caucus","definition":"Sometimes called a caucus, this time-out could be used alone (or with a negotiating partner in a team negotiation) to gather your thoughts, adjust your strategy, and discover new frames or solutions."},{"word":"Conciliator","definition":"is a trusted third party whose role is to ensure that a steady flow of accurate information exists between the negotiating parties."},{"word":"Consultant","definition":"is a neutral third party who teaches and advises the negotiating parties on skills and techniques of negotiation."},{"word":"Distributive bargaining strategy","definition":"is based on an attempt to divide up a fixed \"pie\" or amount of resources, resulting in a win–lose situation."},{"word":"Frame","definition":"is a point of view or perspective we bring to an interaction such as a negotiation."},{"word":"Global negotiations","definition":"With negotiations between domestic and foreign firms becoming more prevalent, it has become more important than ever that in addition to basic negotiation skills, individuals involved in global business have a solid understanding of how different cultures, practices, and customs affect negotiations."},{"word":"Integrative bargaining strategy","definition":"Negotiators use an integrative bargaining strategy when they believe that a win–win situation exists and can be reached."},{"word":"Interest","definition":"is the explanation behind the position, need, or desire that expresses why a negotiator wants what he or she wants."},{"word":"Manageable questions","definition":"tend to keep the conversation moving and toward productive, creative, and supportive ends; appropriate for integrative negotiations."},{"word":"Mediator","definition":"is a neutral third party who has no stake in the outcome of the agreement."},{"word":"Multiparty negotiation","definition":"are those where \"more than two parties are working together to achieve a collective objective.\""},{"word":"Negotiation","definition":"is a process in which two or more people or groups share their concerns and interests to reach an agreement of mutual benefit."},{"word":"Objective criteria","definition":"established norms or methods for assessing the comparative value of options, or a mutually agreed-upon measuring stick."},{"word":"Position","definition":"is a stance—typically a firm one—taken by a negotiator: \"I'll give you $4,500 and that's my final offer.\""},{"word":"Script or strategy outline","definition":"Once you're very clear on your goals and interests, you'll be able to develop a script or strategy outline (see Figure 9–4)."},{"word":"Team-based negotiation","definition":"where each side is represented by at least two individuals who work together as a team to achieve the goals and interests within a negotiation."},{"word":"Third party","definition":"Serious negotiations—such as high-stakes strike threats—often require the use of a third party to gain an agreement. Other times, it is simply a wise idea to bring in an objective third party to help two opposing sides develop a shared agreement."},{"word":"Unmanageable questions","definition":"cause difficulty by bringing the discussion to a false conclusion."},{"word":"Virtual negotiation","definition":"carrying out all or part of a negotiation electronically."}],"title":"Negotiation","id":9},{"vocab":[{"word":"Absentee member","definition":"A member can become distracted by a work or personal problem that prevents him or her from following through on commitments made to the team."},{"word":"Adjourning","definition":"After successfully completing the task or objective, teams may disband permanently or take a temporary break. Some may get new members or receive a new objective. This stage is usually brought on by an imminent deadline."},{"word":"Cross-functional teams","definition":"are usually charged with developing new products or investigating and improving a companywide problem such as the need to increase speed and efficiency across departmental lines or the need to adopt a new companywide computer system."},{"word":"Forming","definition":"In this first stage, a team is established to accomplish a particular task."},{"word":"Group","definition":"is a collection of people who work together but aren't necessarily working collectively toward the same goal."},{"word":"Group-hate","definition":"According to Sorensen, group-hate is the negative view that some people have of working in groups that can influence their active participation in group socialization and group work activities."},{"word":"Groupthink","definition":"individuals agreeing reluctantly with a group's decision because they are more concerned about maintaining harmony and cohesiveness than critically thinking about problems or alternative approaches."},{"word":"High-performance team","definition":"In high-performance teams, there is a commitment to quality and a dedication to producing the best outcome possible."},{"word":"Norming","definition":"In this third stage, the group faces its issues, conflicts, and power and leadership struggles openly and directly."},{"word":"Performing","definition":"In the performing stage, teams have worked through their differences. Their membership is stable, the task is clear, and eyes are on the prize."},{"word":"Process improvement team","definition":"focus on specific methods, operations, or procedures and are assembled with the specific goal of enhancing the particular component being studied."},{"word":"Procrastinator","definition":"We're all human, and a seemingly human tendency is to \"put off until tomorrow what we should be working on today.\""},{"word":"Risky shift","definition":"takes place when members who are part of a group agree to a more risky course of action than any individual would have done alone."},{"word":"Self-managed team","definition":"are \"groups of employees who are responsible for a complete, self-contained package of responsibilities that relate either to a final product or an ongoing process.\""},{"word":"Social loafer","definition":"it is not uncommon for one or more persons on a team to be able to \"hide\" the fact they're not contributing."},{"word":"Social loafing or free riding","definition":"As the number of people working on a task increases, so does the likelihood that one or more members slack off, hoping that others will pick up the slack."},{"word":"Storming","definition":"In this second stage, a group experiences differences over factors such as direction, leadership, work style, and approach, and perceptions about the expected quality and state of the end product or deliverable."},{"word":"Task force","definition":"is an ad hoc, temporary project team assembled to develop a product, service, or system or to solve a specific problem or set of problems."},{"word":"Team","definition":"is a formal work group consisting of people who work together to achieve a common group goal."},{"word":"Virtual teams","definition":"those that operate across time, space, and organizational boundaries using means other than face-to-face meetings."}],"title":"Building Teams and Work Groups","id":10},{"vocab":[{"word":"Accommodating","definition":"When you use an accommodation strategy to resolve a conflict, you are more concerned with maintaining the relationship than with accomplishing a specific goal through the interaction."},{"word":"Avoiding","definition":"In an avoidance or withdrawing strategy, you choose not to deal with the issues or the people involved. You retreat from the situation, hoping it either goes away or resolves itself."},{"word":"Collaborating","definition":"The ultimate \"win–win\" strategy, collaboration involves energy, commitment, and excellent skills in communication, problem solving, and negotiation."},{"word":"Competing","definition":"In a competing strategy, you work to achieve your goals at all costs, even if it means sacrificing the relationship. This is an \"I win, you lose\" strategy."},{"word":"Compromising","definition":"When you compromise or \"split the difference\" in a conflict, you agree to give up part of your goal and part of the relationship in order to reach an agreement."},{"word":"Conflict","definition":"is any situation in which there are incompatible goals, cognitions, or emotions within or between individuals or groups that lead to opposition or antagonistic interaction."},{"word":"Human relations view (of conflict)","definition":"This was the overriding perspective during the 1940–1970 period. In this view, conflict was viewed as a natural and inevitable part of group interaction and relationships. Conflict could at times be functional or dysfunctional, but it was always present."},{"word":"Interactionist View (of conflict)","definition":"This view holds that conflict is inevitable, and can be helpful if some degree of tension is maintained in an effort to keep a group energized, creative, and a positive force for change."},{"word":"Intergroup conflict","definition":"differences between competing subgroups of an organization."},{"word":"Intragroup conflict","definition":"differences between members of one group."},{"word":"Negative or dysfunctional conflict","definition":"hinders the organization's or the person's performance or ability to attain goals or objectives and can lead to stress and anxiety, inability to take action, and loss of esteem or purpose."},{"word":"Positive or functional conflict","definition":"which supports or benefits the organization or person's main objectives."},{"word":"Psychological safety","definition":"refers to the belief that one can share openly and honestly one's true feelings and opinions without risk of retaliation or harm to the relationship."},{"word":"Relationship conflict","definition":"that is based on interpersonal differences is almost always dysfunctional."},{"word":"Task conflict","definition":"conflicts over how to approach a task can be functional."},{"word":"Traditional view (of conflict)","definition":"This view was predominant in the early 20th century when it was believed that conflict was always bad and should be avoided at all costs."}],"title":"Managing Interpersonal and Organizational Conflict","id":11},{"vocab":[{"word":"Agenda","definition":"The meeting agenda (see Figure 12–1 for a sample) clarifies the goal and lists the points of discussion and their priority for the meeting."},{"word":"Calendaring","definition":"the ability to check a colleague's schedule or add meetings to his or her calendar—is now a standard feature in Microsoft Outlook, which is used in many organizations."},{"word":"Electronic messaging or chatting","definition":"Several free and inexpensive Internet-based platforms facilitate synchronous (at the same time) and asynchronous communication and collaboration."},{"word":"Planning and evaluation phase","definition":"This step is often missed in meetings; doing so could negatively impact future meetings. First, determine whether the meeting objective has been met."},{"word":"Information-dissemination meeting","definition":"Critical information is shared with members. Typically, such information is too important for a memo and may require more in-depth explanation or discussion, as in the case of introducing a new reward system."},{"word":"Information-sharing meeting","definition":"Several or all members share information that has been gathered or report on group or individual progress."},{"word":"Meeting kickoff","definition":"Meeting kickoff or the heart of the meeting. If a group of individuals will be meeting on a regular basis, it is important that they establish operating guidelines."},{"word":"Operating guidelines","definition":"or the standard or set of norms to which they will be held, collectively."},{"word":"Preliminary or initial phase","definition":"In the first few minutes of the meeting, clearly articulate the meeting purpose and objectives."},{"word":"Problem-solving/decision-making meeting","definition":"Employees or members are assembled to solve a problem or make a decision."},{"word":"Process check/meeting evaluation","definition":"One way to do this is with a process check, wherein members freely disclose how they felt about what was accomplished and how it was accomplished."},{"word":"Summary of accomplishments","definition":"In this stage, the team leader—with the help of the scribe's notes—will review decisions made and summarize the key points discussed."},{"word":"Symbolic/social meeting","definition":"Employees celebrate a special event or managers share recognition for a job well done."},{"word":"Synergy","definition":"the belief that two heads are better than one."},{"word":"Teleconferencing","definition":"The ability to include multiple members in a meeting via telephone has existed for a long time."},{"word":"Video- and Webconferencing","definition":"Until recently, only very resource-rich organizations could afford the technology to have group videoconferences among employees or between employees and clients in different locations."},{"word":"Virtual meeting","definition":"people from all parts of the organization and world can be efficiently and effectively connected—anytime and anywhere—in virtual meetings."}],"title":"Achieving Business Results through Effective Meetings","id":12},{"vocab":[{"word":"Content","definition":"the content or what the team is doing (e.g., goals, tasks, agenda items, subjects discussed, decisions made)."},{"word":"Dysfunctional behaviors","definition":"are actions taken by members that may hinder or even undermine the team's progress."},{"word":"Facilitation","definition":"is the set of \"activities carried out before, during, and after meetings to help a group achieve its own outcomes.\""},{"word":"Facilitative preventions","definition":"A facilitator can do many things behind the scenes or before meetings begin that might help prevent problems from occurring or diminish their impact on the team should such problems surface."},{"word":"Facilitator or process consultant","definition":"is someone who performs these activities, helping the team by monitoring and improving its internal processes (means)—such as how members communicate, make decisions, or resolve conflict—that are essential for achieving its goals (ends)."},{"word":"Graphic facilitation","definition":"is a powerful facilitation tool that uses both words and pictures to record and facilitate meetings."},{"word":"Intervention","definition":"or things a facilitator can say or do to help a team assess and deal with what is going on in the present moment."},{"word":"Maintenance-related behaviors","definition":"relate to the process of how the group works together."},{"word":"Objectivity","definition":"the ability to view a situation without personal bias."},{"word":"Problem people","definition":"Some people, with or without ill intent, can single-handedly hinder even the best teams. We call these problem people."},{"word":"Process","definition":"The process describes how members go about their task."},{"word":"Task-related behaviors","definition":"focus on the content of the meeting—the actions necessary to complete a task or goal."}],"title":"Facilitating Team Success","id":13},{"vocab":[{"word":"Affinitizing","definition":"provides a useful means to organize and produce agreement on categories of ideas; doing so facilitates a group's ability to address organizational problems."},{"word":"Analogy method","definition":"Fix a problem or create something new by thinking of other products, people, animals, or social units that perform similar acts to make analogies."},{"word":"Analysis paralysis","definition":"We may become overwhelmed by the options and never proceed to selecting one, a condition known as analysis paralysis."},{"word":"Brainstorming","definition":"is a tool used to stimulate and capture creative thoughts and ideas. It involves the creative generation of many ideas to solve a problem."},{"word":"Collaborative decision making","definition":"involves identifying your and the other parties' priorities and determining the option(s) available that meet both sets of needs."},{"word":"Convergent thinking","definition":"is starting with a problem and working to move toward a solution."},{"word":"Creativity","definition":"bringing into existence an idea that is new to you."},{"word":"Decision making","definition":"is a process by which several possibilities are considered and prioritized, resulting in a clear choice of one option over others."},{"word":"Decision-making process","definition":"represents a straightforward decision-making process for almost any type of decision, whether individual or group."},{"word":"Divergent thinking","definition":"involves generating new ways to view a problem and seeking novel alternatives to the problem."},{"word":"Idea growers","definition":"as Goman calls statements like these, really help continue conversations focused on creative problem solving and stimulate further creative ideas."},{"word":"Idea killers","definition":"Goman calls these and similar statements idea killers."},{"word":"Innovation chamber","definition":"set aside a room (or part of another room or basement) and call it the innovation chamber."},{"word":"Multivoting","definition":"can be used with a brainstormed list of topics or a pared-down list of affinitized categories."},{"word":"Nominal group technique (NGT)","definition":"is another variation on open brainstorming that ameliorates the negative impact that status differences may have on a problem-solving group."},{"word":"PDCA cycle","definition":"problem solving is a cyclical process composed of four steps: Plan, Do, Check, and Act (the PDCA cycle)."},{"word":"Post-it Note brainstorming","definition":"participants' brainstormed ideas are written on Post-it Notes—one idea per note—instead of on a flip chart."},{"word":"P-P-C technique","definition":"stands for Positives, Possibilities, and Concerns."},{"word":"Problem solving","definition":"is a cyclical process composed of four steps: Plan, Do, Check, and Act."},{"word":"Round robin","definition":"A structured group technique in which participants take turns contributing ideas or responses in sequence."}],"title":"Making Decisions and Solving Problems Creatively","id":14},{"vocab":[{"word":"Asserting","definition":"present your ideas in a straightforward and professional manner."},{"word":"Bridging","definition":"gain support by listening and by building \"bridges\" or relationships; work to develop coalitions for backing."},{"word":"Coercive power","definition":"Based on a person's ability to punish or harm. Others comply because they want to avoid punishment."},{"word":"Expert power","definition":"Based on a person possessing knowledge or superior skills in an area of expertise. Employees comply because they respect and trust the person's knowledge or skill."},{"word":"Formal power","definition":"This set of power mechanisms or processes are conferred on the basis of one's position or managerial level in the organization and are referred to as position power or formal power. Formal power can include legitimate, reward, and coercive power bases."},{"word":"Impression management","definition":"is a process by which we attempt to influence the reactions and images people have of us and our ideas."},{"word":"Informal power","definition":"Person power, or informal power, comes from the qualities of a person or how others view her."},{"word":"Information management","definition":"involves obtaining useful information and managing the information received so that it is accessible and available as needed."},{"word":"Information power","definition":"the access to vital information and control over its distribution to others."},{"word":"Legitimate power","definition":"Based on a person holding a formal position or title. Employees comply because they accept the legitimacy of the person in charge."},{"word":"Locus of control","definition":"is related to a person's belief regarding how much control he or she has over external events or the external environment."},{"word":"Machiavellianism","definition":"A Machiavellian individual uses any means possible for personal gain. Individuals who rate high on this trait believe that the end justifies the means, so any behavior is acceptable if it furthers his or her position, including questionable or unethical behaviors."},{"word":"Managing up","definition":"demonstrate how your ideas or suggestions will benefit the organization; how the proposed changes will blend with the goals of your superior and the company."},{"word":"Organizational politics","definition":"also referred to as politicking, is the use of power and information to move resources toward preferred objectives."},{"word":"Negotiating","definition":"compromise or make concessions; assess and adjust your needs to fit with the needs of others."},{"word":"Person power","definition":"Person power, or informal power, comes from the qualities of a person or how others view her."},{"word":"Politicking","definition":"is advocating for your interests in a way that meets your and your company's objectives."},{"word":"Position power","definition":"This set of power mechanisms or processes are conferred on the basis of one's position or managerial level in the organization and are referred to as position power or formal power."},{"word":"Referent power","definition":"Based on a person's ability to influence because of others' desire to identify or associate with him or her. Others comply because they like, believe in, or want to emulate the person and what he or she represents."},{"word":"Reward power","definition":"Based on a person's ability to provide (or withhold) rewards. Others comply because they desire the rewards available from the person in power."}],"title":"Effective and Ethical Use of Power and Influence","id":15},{"vocab":[{"word":"Career functions","definition":"are aspects of the relationship that enhance career advancement such as sponsorship, exposure, visibility, coaching, protection, challenging assignments, and career strategizing."},{"word":"Co-mentoring","definition":"Co-mentors are a pair of close, collegial friends who are committed to facilitating each other's development and who take turns mentoring each other at particular stages of their careers/lives."},{"word":"Formal mentors","definition":"(also called organizational mentors) are often arranged through a prescribed mentoring program, and are typically matched to a protégé with the assistance of an external organizing force (for example, human resources)."},{"word":"Group mentoring","definition":"Some mentoring groups are formally arranged by organizations (especially where there is a shortage of mentors), for example, the director of finance mentors a group of young financial analysts."},{"word":"Informal mentors","definition":"These relationships often develop spontaneously (within or outside an organization) and when a mentor and protégé find each other (either when a potential protégé seeks another's advice or a potential mentor notices another's potential and offers to take the protégé under his or her wing) and negotiate the terms of their relationship."},{"word":"Information interviews","definition":"During an information interview, the goal is to learn from the person with whom you're speaking, not to obtain a job."},{"word":"Mentor","definition":"a mentor (generally a higher-ranking employee with advanced experience in the organization or industry)."},{"word":"Mentoring","definition":"has been traditionally defined as a long-term, developmental relationship between two individuals."},{"word":"Networking","definition":"is the building and nurturing of personal and professional relationships to create a system or chain of information, contacts, and support."},{"word":"Peer mentoring","definition":"is a relationship between group members at the same level. Typically informal, these relationships tend to provide greater levels of psychosocial support than instrumental support."},{"word":"Primary network","definition":"those individuals you know personally."},{"word":"Protégé","definition":"a protégé (generally a lower-ranking employee who receives and benefits from the advice, coaching, friendship, and access to career opportunities a mentor provides)."},{"word":"Psychosocial functions","definition":"are the aspects of the relationship that enhance a sense of competency, identity, and effectiveness in a professional role, including role modeling, acceptance and confirmation, counseling, friendship, support, and personal feedback."},{"word":"Reverse networking","definition":"Employees are offered rewards for helping the company hire new (even seasonal) employees, and the reward grows if the person remains with the company beyond a certain number of months. Also called reverse networking, this practice offers numerous benefits to firms over traditional recruiting strategies."},{"word":"Secondary network","definition":"If each of your 250 direct contacts has a personal network of 250, the first tier alone will have over 62,500 contacts. This is referred to as the secondary network, and its existence indicates the importance of not relying solely on those you know when doing research, getting to know an organization, or searching for a job."},{"word":"Social capital","definition":"the resources that can provide new ideas, timely information, job opportunities, business leads, influence, and social support as a result of these relationships."},{"word":"Virtual mentors","definition":"consider e-mentoring (using email, phone, or videoconference to initiate and build critical developmental relationships)."}],"title":"Networking and Mentoring","id":16},{"vocab":[{"word":"Asking for feedback","definition":"By asking for feedback, we demonstrate our commitment to improve and our dedication to do things right."},{"word":"Coaching","definition":"Coaching involves guiding, instructing, and training, with a focus on teaching individuals, groups, or teams specific skills needed to improve their performance. Coaching is a means for managers to provide their employees with guidance, insight, and encouragement through frequent interactions in order to improve their work performance."},{"word":"Counseling","definition":"is used to address personal or attitudinal problems rather than those related to an individual's ability (or lack thereof)."},{"word":"Descriptive","definition":"To give descriptive feedback, focus your feedback on a specific behavior that the recipient can change or control, describing behavior rather than evaluating it."},{"word":"Directive methods","definition":"include probing, questioning, and discussing specific problems and possible solutions."},{"word":"Documentable","definition":"When giving specific work-related feedback, make sure it is based on fact rather than hearsay. Also keep a written record of any conversations you have when using feedback to help employees correct undesirable behaviors."},{"word":"Feedback","definition":"is information that enables individuals or groups to compare actual performance with a given standard or expectation."},{"word":"Giving feedback","definition":"Giving feedback greatly benefits those with whom you work. When information about performance is given in such a way that the person can learn and grow from the feedback, it can be an enormous boost for professional confidence and competence."},{"word":"Initiating behaviors","definition":"Initiating and structuring actions and discussions: providing feedback and analysis of issues and concerns; clarifying leader expectations and requirements; exploring impact and effects of employee's actions; planning actions around solutions and desired changes; seeking commitment to the action plan; clarifying positive and negative consequences connected to future action and plans."},{"word":"Nondirective approach","definition":"involves being a good listener and sounding board."},{"word":"Nonprescriptive","definition":"Give nonprescriptive feedback. Avoid moralizing or giving feedback that is judgmental or prescriptive."},{"word":"Receiving constructive feedback","definition":"When receiving constructive feedback, it is important to demonstrate an openness to hearing the information and benefiting from it."},{"word":"Supportive behaviors","definition":"A leader's words and actions showing concern and acceptance of employees: collaboration regarding solutions to problems; help and assistance through training and resources; concern over the employee's needs and objectives; empathy for the employee and attention to obstacles and problems; expressions of the value of the employee and his or her contribution to the work; acceptance of responsibility in situations; interaction that provides time for the employee to air his or her feelings."}],"title":"Coaching and Providing Feedback for Improved Performance","id":17},{"vocab":[{"word":"Accountability","definition":"Delegation is not complete without holding delegatees accountable for the completion of the assignments. They need to be aware of the rewards and consequences of their actions and must realize that they will have to justify decisions for the tasks for which they are responsible."},{"word":"Assignment of responsibility","definition":"When assigning the responsibility to accomplish a task, the delegatee must understand exactly what is to be accomplished and accept the responsibility for doing so."},{"word":"Authority","definition":"The delegatee must have the proper authority to obtain the necessary resources to complete the task."},{"word":"Delegation","definition":"Delegation involves assigning work—and the authority and responsibility for the work—to others."},{"word":"Empowerment","definition":"In simple terms, empowerment is the process by which a leader or manager shares his or her power with subordinates."},{"word":"Hierarchy of needs theory","definition":"Maslow's hierarchy of needs theory proposes that people are intrinsically motivated in direct relationship to their needs, from the most basic lower-level needs (physiological, safety) progressing up the hierarchy to the social or higher-level needs (social, self-esteem, self-actualization)."},{"word":"High involvement","definition":"Employees in a high involvement organization have much greater voice and discretion over their work environment."},{"word":"Implementation stage","definition":"Implementation—assessing all current systems and adjusting to support an environment based on empowerment."},{"word":"Investigation stage","definition":"Investigation—analyzing whether empowerment should be implemented and in what form."},{"word":"Job involvement","definition":"In the job involvement approach to empowerment, employees are given greater freedom in their job and tasks."},{"word":"Knowledge worker","definition":"Knowledge workers are employees who need and use information to perform their work."},{"word":"Leadership","definition":"A process of social influence to move individuals and groups toward goal achievement. Leadership is not a rank. It is a responsibility."},{"word":"Maturation stage","definition":"Maturation—the new system is firmly rooted."},{"word":"Preparation stage","definition":"Preparation—setting the stage for generating and demonstrating organizational support."},{"word":"Self-leadership","definition":"Self-leadership is what happens when individuals act on their own to achieve the organization's mission, vision, purpose, values, and goals."},{"word":"Suggestion involvement","definition":"In suggestion involvement, the organization makes a small shift from the production line or control model. Employees are encouraged to contribute ideas, possibly via an anonymous suggestion box."},{"word":"Theory X manager","definition":"A Theory X manager believes subordinates dislike work and shirk responsibility, leading them to be more directive with the tendency to dictate work efforts."},{"word":"Theory Y manager","definition":"An organization predominated by participative, open Theory Y managers—holding the belief that people enjoy work, crave responsibility, and strive for excellence—is the perfect environment for an empowerment approach to problem solving."},{"word":"Transition stage","definition":"Transition—moving from the former system to the new one."}],"title":"Leading and Empowering Self and Others","id":18},{"vocab":[{"word":"Backscheduling","definition":"This process, called backscheduling, involves looking backward from a target date, beginning with your goal or objective, and then plotting the means to achieve it."},{"word":"Critical path method (CPM)","definition":"The PERT process can also determine the longest anticipated single line of activity from start to finish, which is known as the critical path method (CPM). The critical path is the longest path through the networks to complete the project."},{"word":"Fishbone diagram","definition":"Using a \"cause and effect\" diagram such as Ishikawa's fishbone diagram is helpful in early stages of project management to gather and organize project factors and in finding hidden issues that may impact activities, resource allocation, or steps in the overall process."},{"word":"Gantt chart","definition":"One of the more common and simple tools is the Gantt chart. Named after its developer, Henry Gantt, this chart describes the temporal relationships of events or tasks that unfold over time."},{"word":"Program evaluation and review technique (PERT) chart","definition":"A PERT chart diagrams all the steps involved in completing a project and estimates the length of time needed in each phase of the project."},{"word":"Project management","definition":"is the coordination of your work and that of others such that organizational objectives can be achieved while meeting time, budget, and quality standards or expectations."},{"word":"Project time line","definition":"Some tactics for developing a project time line or work plan include these: working with a large blackboard, whiteboard, easel and newsprint, or computer program (something that everyone involved can see), begin brainstorming all the steps that will be needed to complete the project."},{"word":"Resource management","definition":"Be realistic about what can be accomplished given the resources available and time constraints inherent in the project; this will facilitate effective resource management over the course of the project."},{"word":"Stakeholders","definition":"the fact that their work or organization is affected by the outcomes of the project, thereby making them stakeholders, may suggest that their inclusion is more important than you might think."},{"word":"Work plan","definition":"Figure 19–3 shows a work plan for a five-member team presentation project. It includes all the steps necessary to complete the project, a time frame to allow for completion of the project, and assignment of responsibilities for individual team members."}],"title":"Project Management","id":19}];
